          "A6": {
            "value": "Commentary",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A7": {
            "value": "Asian Paints' WACC (11.65%) is nearly identical to its Ke (11.73%) because the company has minimal leverage; debt represents only 1.34% of total capital, so the weighted cost of debt contribution is negligible. Reliance's WACC (13.27%) is substantially below its Ke (13.23%) despite having a higher Ke than Asian Paints, because Reliance's 16.67% debt-to-capital weight combined with a lower after-tax cost of debt (4.63%) creates a powerful drag that reduces the blended cost. IndiGo's WACC (13.74%) would be significantly higher (~15.8%) if lease obligations were ignored; by capitalizing the ₹110 cr operating lease liability, we properly reflect IndiGo's true fixed obligations and reduce apparent equity risk. This teaches that capital structure fundamentally shapes WACC: a company with high leverage and cheap debt (like Reliance) can have a much lower cost of capital than an unlevered peer despite a higher equity risk premium, while companies with hidden leverage (like IndiGo's leases) must capitalize those obligations to avoid systematically overstating the weighted average cost of capital and undervaluing the firm.",
            "readOnly": true
          }
        }
